  "query_preview": "/WEDNESDAY THROUGH SUNDAY/...\nIssued at 310 AM CDT Mon Aug 3 2026\n\nWednesday, the cold front attendant to the upper level system begins \nto swing down through Missouri. The surface low that has been \nhanging out to our west joins forces with it, and we can expect rain \nacross much of the area. SPC has drawn a Marginal risk spanning from \neastern Kansas through central Missouri into west-central Illinois \nincluding counties in northern and western portions of our CWA. \nGiven that this is a Day 3 risk, it is likely to change both in area \nand severity. The modulating factors are instability, which is \nuncertain due to cloud cover, and front positioning, which may be \nimpacted by thunderstorm outflow on its way to us. If the \ninstability and lift can line up, the deep-layer shear looks \nsufficient, as well as parallel to the front. This preliminarily \nsuggests a line or line segments as the primary threat, meaning we \nmay be looking at severe potential in the way of damaging winds. \nThese storms would quickly outrun the strong deep-layer shear as \nthey move to the southeast, which is why most of the area is \noutlooked in \"general thunder\" with the small Marginal in the north.\n\nCurrently, timing-wise, things look to start approaching the CWA \naround sunrise. Progression is slow, and most may not see much \nduring the day. The front finally dives deeper into the CWA \nWednesday evening into Thursday morning. Shortwave energy behind the \nmain trough along with surface low pressure to the southwest push \nmore rain into the area even after the main front has \nstalled/retreated, keeping Thursday fairly rainy with PoPs 40-60%. \nThe activity lingers into Friday, slowly moving out to the southeast.\n\nWith temperatures dampened a bit during the rainy days, southwest \nflow Saturday paired with sunny skies helps to kick the warming \ntrend back into gear. Heat Index values will climb once again, \nbreaking 100 for some. Above-average temperatures seem to make a \nreturn heading into mid-August, and we will need to keep an eye out \nfor Extreme Heat once again.",
